# Independently authored PG EXPLAIN no-option-registry +
# parens-vs-top-level grammar asymmetry sentinel slice
# for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted
# from MySQL's mysql-test/ or PG regress.
#
# THIRD "no registry" witness in the series:
#   - PR #458 — pg_create_function_language_params_sentinel
#     pinned that CREATE FUNCTION accepts ANY LANGUAGE tag
#     including `LANGUAGE nosuchlang`. No per-tag registry.
#   - PR #459 — pg_database_no_option_registry_sentinel
#     pinned that CREATE/ALTER DATABASE accepts EVERY option
#     keyword including `NOSUCHOPT` and the entire fabricated
#     option set. No per-option registry.
#   - THIS SLICE — EXPLAIN's parenthesized option list
#     accepts EVERY option name including `NOSUCHOPT`,
#     ALL PG13+/16+/17+ options, and arbitrary 6-option
#     chains. No per-option registry. The third witness
#     confirms a STRUCTURAL pattern, not a one-off oversight.
#
# Adjacent EXPLAIN coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - explain_basic_extra (PR earlier) — pins SeqScan /
#     HashJoin / UPDATE / DELETE plan-tree shapes, PG-style
#     `EXPLAIN (VERBOSE)` no-op stability, and the MySQL
#     `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON` no-op stability. Does NOT cover
#     PG13+/16+/17+ options or the no-registry headline.
#   - explain_format_basic_extra — pins `(FORMAT YAML)`,
#     `(FORMAT XML)`, `(FORMAT JUNK)`, `(ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`,
#     `(BUFFERS)`, `(COSTS OFF)`, `(SETTINGS)`, plus the
#     `EXPLAIN ANALYZE INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE` 1105 errno path.
#     `(FORMAT JUNK)` is the closest prior witness to
#     "arbitrary option name accepted" — BUT it only proves
#     that the FORMAT-arg slot is unvalidated, not that the
#     option-keyword slot itself is registry-less.
#   - pg_explain_format_silent_drop_bug_sentinel — pins the
#     CORRUPTION-class angle: `(FORMAT JSON)` returns TEXT
#     plan, not JSON. Different finding — that's a SILENT-
#     DROP of a valid option, not a no-registry accept of
#     an INVALID option name.
#
# Pre-author dedup grep on tests/main/*.test for
# `EXPLAIN.*\(.*(GENERIC_PLAN|MEMORY|SUMMARY|TIMING|WAL|
# NOSUCHOPT)|EXPLAIN ANALYZE GENERIC|EXPLAIN ANALYZE,`:
# ZERO hits. Greenfield slice.
#
# This slice pins THREE structural findings:
#
#   FINDING 1 — THIRD NO-REGISTRY WITNESS. EXPLAIN's
#   parenthesized option list silently accepts ANY
#   identifier as an option name, including the fabricated
#   `NOSUCHOPT` (Shape A10 — the headline). This is the
#   exact same mechanism witnessed in PR #458 (LANGUAGE
#   tag) and PR #459 (CREATE/ALTER DATABASE options): the
#   parser absorbs the identifier into the AST as a free-
#   form token and the IR-conversion layer never consults
#   a per-option registry. Three independent statement
#   types (CREATE FUNCTION, CREATE/ALTER DATABASE, EXPLAIN)
#   share the same no-registry shape — this is a
#   structural pattern in zeta's parser, not a per-
#   statement oversight. Apps that rely on PG's strict
#   option-name validation (e.g., tooling that
#   programmatically generates EXPLAIN options based on
#   user input) will silently see the bad option NOT
#   error, but ALSO not take effect — see Finding 2.
#
#   FINDING 2 — PG13+/16+/17+ FEATURE VISIBILITY GAP.
#   Five real PG options that were added in PG13 or
#   later — GENERIC_PLAN (PG16+), MEMORY (PG17+), SUMMARY
#   (PG13+), TIMING (PG13+), WAL (PG13+) — all silently
#   accept WITHOUT producing the metrics the user
#   requested. Output is the same `SeqScan (e) [id, v]` +
#   cost row that bare EXPLAIN emits (Shape C). For PG
#   users porting tooling that relies on SUMMARY/TIMING/
#   MEMORY/WAL metrics for performance triage (the entire
#   reason those options were added), the queries return
#   NO error and NO data — a CORRUPTION-class failure
#   mode for performance-tuning workflows. Shape A11
#   (6-option chain `ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE, COSTS
#   OFF, SUMMARY, TIMING`) demonstrates that even
#   real-world combinations from PG documentation
#   examples silently degrade to bare-EXPLAIN output.
#
#   FINDING 3 — PARENS-vs-TOP-LEVEL GRAMMAR ASYMMETRY.
#   The top-level (no parens) EXPLAIN form has a STRICT
#   whitelist limited to ANALYZE and VERBOSE keywords;
#   any other keyword and any comma-separator triggers a
#   1064 parse error. Shape B1 (`EXPLAIN ANALYZE
#   GENERIC_PLAN SELECT * FROM e`) errors at Column 30
#   — `GENERIC_PLAN` is absorbed as an identifier after
#   `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`, then SELECT chokes at the next
#   token. Shape B2 (`EXPLAIN ANALYZE, BUFFERS SELECT *
#   FROM e`) errors at Column 16 — the comma after
#   ANALYZE is not allowed at top level (commas are
#   parens-only). TWO DIFFERENT PARSER CODE PATHS for
#   the same EXPLAIN feature: the parens form has no
#   registry at all (accepts everything), the top-level
#   form has a tight whitelist (rejects everything but
#   ANALYZE/VERBOSE). This asymmetry pairs with
#   pre-existing patterns like
#   `[[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]]` (the
#   type parser and CAST dispatcher are out of sync) —
#   same feature, different fingerprints, different
#   paths.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   The "no registry" pattern is now confirmed across
#   THREE independent statement families. zeta's parser
#   has a consistent design choice (or oversight) of
#   absorbing optional keyword lists as free-form
#   identifiers without per-keyword validation. Combined
#   with PR #458, PR #459 and this slice, the pattern is
#   structural: any future statement that takes a
#   parenthesized option list should be assumed to have
#   no registry until proven otherwise.
#
#   The parens-vs-top-level asymmetry in EXPLAIN adds a
#   second axis: even WITHIN a single statement type,
#   different syntactic shapes for the same feature can
#   take entirely different parser paths with entirely
#   different validation policies. PG porting tools that
#   verify "EXPLAIN works" by probing the top-level form
#   will get a STRICTER answer than reality — apps
#   actually use the parens form (the only form PG
#   recommends since 9.0), which has zero validation.
#
# Wire-divergence check:
#   This slice is mysqlwire-canonical (the MTR runner is
#   the only probe). The EXPLAIN-option-list parser sits
#   in the shared statement parser, so pgwire is
#   expected to exhibit the same no-registry behavior;
#   not independently confirmed here.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: NO EXPLAIN OPTION REGISTRY — the
#     parenthesized `EXPLAIN (...)` option list silently
#     accepts ANY identifier as an option name, including
#     `NOSUCHOPT` and all PG13+/16+/17+ options
#     (GENERIC_PLAN, MEMORY, SUMMARY, TIMING, WAL). The
#     options have no effect — output is identical to
#     bare `EXPLAIN`. NO error, NO warning. Apps relying
#     on PG's strict option validation MUST validate
#     option names themselves before issuing against
#     zeta. THIRD "no registry" witness after PR #458
#     (LANGUAGE tag) and PR #459 (DATABASE options).
#   # zeta-adapt: PARENS-vs-TOP-LEVEL ASYMMETRY — the
#     top-level (no parens) EXPLAIN form has a strict
#     whitelist (ANALYZE, VERBOSE only). Other keywords
#     and commas at top level error 1064. The parens
#     form has no whitelist at all. Two parser paths,
#     two validation policies, one feature.

--echo === Setup ===

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS e;
CREATE TABLE e (id INT PRIMARY KEY, v INT);
INSERT INTO e VALUES (1, 10), (2, 20);

--echo === SHAPE A — parenthesized EXPLAIN: ANY option silently accepted (no registry) ===

# All 11 rows return the SAME `SeqScan (e) [id, v]` +
# cost row that bare EXPLAIN emits (see Shape C). The
# parenthesized option lists are evaluated as a noop —
# the actual plan output is unchanged from the baseline.

--echo === Shape A1: (GENERIC_PLAN) — PG16+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A2: (GENERIC_PLAN TRUE) — boolean-arg form silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN TRUE) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A3: (MEMORY) — PG17+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (MEMORY) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A4: (SUMMARY) — PG13+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (SUMMARY) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A5: (SUMMARY TRUE) — boolean-arg form silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (SUMMARY TRUE) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A6: (TIMING) — PG13+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (TIMING) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A7: (WAL) — PG13+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (WAL) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A8: (WAL, ANALYZE) — multi-option list silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (WAL, ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A9: (FORMAT YAML, MEMORY ON) — FORMAT + PG17+ option silently accepted ===

EXPLAIN (FORMAT YAML, MEMORY ON) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A10: (NOSUCHOPT) — HEADLINE: fabricated option name silently accepted ===

# The MOST DAMNING shape in the slice — `NOSUCHOPT` is
# a fabricated identifier that does not exist in any PG
# version. zeta accepts it without error. PROOF that no
# per-option registry exists; the parser absorbs the
# token as a free-form identifier and the IR-conversion
# layer never reads the field. This is the third
# "NOSUCHOPT silently accepted" finding in the series
# after PR #458 (LANGUAGE nosuchlang) and PR #459
# (DATABASE NOSUCHOPT).

EXPLAIN (NOSUCHOPT) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape A11: 6-option chain — every real PG option silently accepted ===

# Real-world PG documentation example shape. ALL SIX
# options silently noop. Output identical to bare
# EXPLAIN — no ANALYZE side-effect (query NOT executed),
# no BUFFERS metrics, no VERBOSE detail, COSTS still
# shown (not OFF), no SUMMARY footer, no TIMING column.
# CORRUPTION-class for PG users porting performance-
# triage workflows.

EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, VERBOSE, COSTS OFF, SUMMARY, TIMING) SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === SHAPE B — top-level (no parens) form: grammar asymmetry, strict whitelist ===

# The top-level form rejects every keyword and separator
# that the parens form accepts. Two parser code paths,
# two validation policies. Apps probing only the top-
# level form get a STRICTER picture than the real one.

--echo === Shape B1: top-level GENERIC_PLAN — 1064 at SELECT (Column 30) ===

# `GENERIC_PLAN` (12 chars) is absorbed as an identifier
# after `EXPLAIN ANALYZE ` (16 chars including space).
# Position: 16 + 12 + 1 (space) = Column 29, SELECT at
# Column 30. The next token (SELECT) chokes because the
# top-level form is now in "expecting end-of-statement"
# state after consuming the trailing identifier.

--error 1064
EXPLAIN ANALYZE GENERIC_PLAN SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Shape B2: top-level comma — 1064 at comma (Column 16) ===

# Position: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` is 15 chars, comma at
# Column 16. The top-level parser is in "expecting
# identifier" state after ANALYZE (probably trying to
# parse a second top-level keyword like VERBOSE); the
# comma is rejected because commas are valid ONLY
# inside the parens form.

--error 1064
EXPLAIN ANALYZE, BUFFERS SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === SHAPE C — SUPPORT: bare EXPLAIN baseline (re-pin from explain_basic_extra) ===

# Re-pinned to make the no-registry story self-contained:
# every Shape A row produces the EXACT SAME output as
# this baseline. The option clauses are evaluated as a
# pure noop.

EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM e;

--echo === Cleanup ===

DROP TABLE e;
